{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d like to thank Speakly for supporting\nPBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2640,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein once asked if “the moon exists\nonly when I look at it?\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7740,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was rhetorical objection to the idea that\nmeasurement in quantum mechanics causes reality"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12900,
    "dDurationMs": 900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to become real."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14520,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there was a time when the moon didn’t\nexist, and then hours later suddenly did."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19440,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least, according to the latest simulations\nof its formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29040,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No one was there to see the Moon form, but\nlet’s side with Einstein and assume that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33660,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this was a real event that actually happened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36300,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Without direct observation, we have to\nget clever to figure out what went down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40200,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there’s good reason to be curious."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42120,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s moon is very, very unusual."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45420,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing, it’s gigantic - by far the\nlargest relative to its planet in the whole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50220,
    "dDurationMs": 600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solar system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52140,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Excluding Pluto, which isn’t a planet anymore\nso whatever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56640,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The moon's composition is also unusual - it \nhas a relatively tiny iron core for its size."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62100,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, the elements that it does\nhave look like they could have been scraped"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66720,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off the surface of the Earth, which you would\nabsolutely not expect for a celestial body"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70980,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that wasn’t scraped off the surface of the\nEarth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74400,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get into how such a body could have\nformed, let’s talk about how we even know"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79740,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of this, and why these properties of the\nmoon are so unusual."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84660,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There was this magical time half a century\nago - it wasn’t very long, just a few years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90480,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- when humans actually visited the moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94560,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Apollo program may have had a political\nside, but it also provided a trove of scientific"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100200,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data about our celestial little sibling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103500,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, seismometers were set up at every\nlanding site, which allowed geologists to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109680,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tune into the moon’s vibrations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111480,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Impacts from asteroids or even the spent fuel\nstage of the Apollo 17 Saturn V rocket cause"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118920,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seismic waves - moonquakes. They resonated through\nthe lunar interior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124680,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the nature of the waves that reach the\nseismometer we can reconstruct the moon’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130380,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130860,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it turns out the insides \nof the moon are weird."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135300,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most notably, its inner core of iron is really "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139020,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "small, only about 20% of the \nmoon's diameter - surrounded"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143400,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by a long-since frozen mantle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145500,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By comparison, Earth’s core is closer to\n50% of its diameter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150300,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Apollo astronauts brought back a half-ton\nof material from the Moon’s surface, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155700,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "adds the second layer to the mystery."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each element on the periodic table can have\ndifferent numbers of neutrons - they come"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163440,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in different isotopes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164940,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ratio of isotopes for a given element\nvaried across the protoplanetary disk that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171420,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our solar system formed from."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173820,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each world in our system has a signature set\nof isotopic ratios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178620,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mars is different to Earth is different to\nsome random asteroid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183480,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in the Apollo samples, isotopes of oxygen\nin the lunar silicate rocks are identical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189300,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to those on Earth to a few parts per million."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192300,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK. So the moon has an isotopic composition\nidentical to Earth’s crust, but it has an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "abundance of heavy elements that’s very\ndifferent, evidenced by its tiny iron core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205500,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re going to need more data."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207720,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we can get that by just looking at the\nmoon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211140,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The types of rock on the lunar surface also\nspeak to its history."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214320,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The moon clearly has darker stuff and lighter\nstuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217560,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The dark regions are basaltic flows, where\nvolcanic magma oozed onto the surface and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223740,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "froze, similar to the dark rocks of Hawaii\nand Iceland."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227820,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These so-called ‘mare,’ Latin for ‘seas’,\nfill many relatively flat impact craters in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235080,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lowlands, mostly on the near side of the moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238200,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meanwhile, the light spots are anorthositic\nrocks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241380,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is also an igneous rock like basalt,\nbut it forms differently."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246900,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Basalt forms ‘extrusively’, solidifying "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250080,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the surface, while \nanorthosites form ‘intrusively’"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254100,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inside the magna."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They are made of light elements like calcium,\nsilicon, and oxygen and are less dense than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261600,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the magma that makes them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262860,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because of this, when bits of anorthosite\nstart to freeze within the still-molten magma"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269040,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they tend to float to the top."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272100,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Together, these rocks tell us that the moon\nwas once covered by a layer of liquid magma"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277560,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- and that would have been for tens to hundreds\nof millions of years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281400,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over that time it would have formed a light\nanorthositic crust that was later splotched"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287520,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by darker basalt as eruptions pushed some\nmagma back up on top."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291660,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then to finish it off, add a couple billion\nyears of impact craters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296820,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So then, how do we make a gigantic moon from\nEarth-like material with a really dinky core"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302760,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and completely covered with a magma ocean?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305220,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s talk about some of the ways to make a \nmoon, and see if we can find one that we like."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311340,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe the most obvious way is to do it like\nwe make planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315360,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planets pull themselves together gravitationally\nout of the protoplanetary disk leftover from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321900,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun’s formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323520,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in that process they have \ntheir own ‘circumplanetary "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327300,
    "dDurationMs": 600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disks’."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327900,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this environment, planets and moons grow\nsimultaneously until the disk is exhausted,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334200,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and this is the kind of \nformation we expect produced "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337620,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the big moons of Jupiter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339480,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This mode of formation would explain the matching\nisotopic ratios of the Earth and moon, because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345120,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they both would have formed from the same\ngeneral region of the protoplanetary disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349980,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if this were the whole story then we’d "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352800,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expect the moon to have \nroughly the same proportion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355140,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of different elements, not just the same isotope\nratios for a given element."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359880,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the moon’s tiny core tells us this is\nnot the case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363780,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another piece of evidence against this formation\nmechanism is that the rotation axis of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369420,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earth and the plane of the moon’s orbit\ndon’t line up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373020,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If they formed from the same \ndisk, they should line up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377760,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so the moon probably did not form in a \ncircumplanetary disk at the same time as the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382500,
    "dDurationMs": 120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383220,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what else we got?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385800,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you don’t make your moon at home, the\nother way to get one is to order out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390240,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Plenty of moons in the solar system show evidence\nof forming elsewhere before being gravitationally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396180,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "captured by their planet But that’s harder\nthan it sounds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399600,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The biggest challenge is slowing down your\npotential moon during a close flyby and stopping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405420,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it from just whizzing right back off into\nspace."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408240,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can do that with atmospheric drag, and\nthe gas giants capture asteroids that way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413640,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s also a possible explanation for Mars’s\nsad little potato moons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418860,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this is pretty implausible for a moon\nlike ours, which completely dwarfs our atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424140,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another way to capture a moon is to drop in\ntwo at the same time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427800,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The complicated gravitational ballet of a\nbinary - or really trinary system - can leave"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434940,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one body in orbit and kick the other out with\neven higher velocity than it entered with."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440040,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This sort of ‘exchange’ was probably the \nsource Neptune’s moon Triton, with its huge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445980,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tilt and backwards orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448380,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Capture was actually one of the most popular \nexplanations for our moon’s origin until,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454020,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you guessed it, the Apollo missions killed\nit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456540,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those isotopic ratios really are a smoking \ngun for the moon and earth formed from the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463080,
    "dDurationMs": 600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same stuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464580,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how did it form from the same stuff if\nit didn’t form in Earth’s circumplanetary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470640,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disk?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471120,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The last, most likely scenario, and by \nfar the most awesome scenario is the giant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478080,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impact hypothesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479460,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The basic idea is that two proto-planets were\nforming in the same orbit and collided."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486540,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There was a larger one that \nwe’ll call Proto-earth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489960,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and probably a Mars-ish sized body that we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493740,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "call Theia after the mother of the moon in\nGreek mythology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497520,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Theia most likely formed at the L4 or L5 Lagrange\npoint, leading or trailing proto-earth by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504840,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a sixth of an orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507120,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the solar system was about 100 million\nyears old and the planets had already eaten"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512040,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up most of their raw materials, Theia’s "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514380,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit destabilized – probably \nfrom the gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517860,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tugs of the other planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519600,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It began to drift toward the earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522600,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s take a moment to imagine what that\nwould have looked like … A giant planet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529320,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "getting bigger and bigger and bigger on the\nsky until…"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533760,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, for decency let’s zoom out and look\nat a simulation of what may have happened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540360,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Theia smashes into proto-Earth, and the debris\nforms a disk around the liquified planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547320,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Much of that debris eventually rains back\nonto the Earth, but some recedes outward and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551940,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over months to years pulls itself together\ninto the moon with its own gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556500,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The giant impact hypothesis solves a lot of\nour problems – the isotopic similarity of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561840,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the earth and moon would come from mixing\nduring the impact."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566220,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The moon’s iron core is small because the\nlighter stuff from both planets got sprayed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571500,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into space, while Theia’s iron core was\nabsorbed by the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575880,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And a collision like this would have easily\nliquefied rock, giving us our lunar magma"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581460,
    "dDurationMs": 300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ocean."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581760,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Besides explaining the anomalies of the moon,\nthis hypothesis gives us some bonus answers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587400,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the Earth’s own weirdnesses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590160,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth has a particularly robust iron core,\nwhich is responsible for our planet’s strong"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595260,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protective magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596520,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Absorbing Theia’s core may have really helped\nout there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600660,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally, Earth’s own rotational axis should\nhave started out the same as its orbital axis,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606660,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but now it’s tilted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608640,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This collision would have been more than enough\nto knock our tilt off axis, depending on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614280,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "angle of impact."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615000,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This story seems to fit a lot of the data,\nbut there are lots of uncertainties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620160,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What were the real masses of the bodies?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622860,
    "dDurationMs": 1080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their relative speeds?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623940,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Was it a glancing hit, or a head-on collision?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625920,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And how do you even test something like this?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629820,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While we can’t go and smash proto-planets\ninto each other to test these different scenarios"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634860,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- at least not in the real universe … yet\n- we can obliterate worlds to our hearts content"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642300,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in computer simulations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644040,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Using massive hydrodynamic simulations run\non supercomputers, scientists explore the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650940,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wide range of outcomes for different giant\nimpact scenarios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a given setup for a virtual collision between\na proto-Earth and Theia leads to something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660600,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the Earth-moon system, then it’s a\nsign we’re on the right track."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665520,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Up until now, typical simulations have played\nout like I described - Theia crashes into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671640,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the moon, spraying up a big tail of debris\nthat splashes around the earth and and also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676740,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forms a big disk in orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678600,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over months to years that forms the moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681960,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While we can test pretty much any scenario\nthis way, there’s still a big limitation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687180,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- and that's the resolution of the simulation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690000,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can’t simulate every atom, or even every\npebble, but we can approximate the system"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696120,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as say hundreds of thousands of particles \nall interacting gravitationally, and-or fluid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702180,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cells interacting hydrodynamically."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704520,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can have confidence in the result of these\napproximations if increasing the resolution"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710580,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn’t change the outcome too much."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713400,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But apparently, that’s not the case yet,\nbecause a very new simulation at a vastly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719340,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "higher resolution has found something completely\ndifferent to anything we’ve seen before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725100,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earlier this year, Dr. Jacob Kegerreis at\nNASA Ames and Durham University in the UK"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730380,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and his collaborators ran simulations with\nas many as a hundred million little matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735840,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles, a thousand times more detailed\nthan standard simulations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740280,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s take a look at their best simulation\nto see what they discovered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We starts with a pretty typical scenario-\na very nearly earth-sized proto-earth, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750720,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a roughly Mars-sized Theia."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752340,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The collision happens at about a 45 degree\nangle at very nearly the escape velocity-"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758160,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not a head on collision, but still a major\ncollision."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762180,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While the proto-earth survives the collision,\nTheia is basically obliterated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766860,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The debris from Theia shears an enormous amount\nof the early earth’s mantle off with it,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772500,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "creating a plume towering thousands of kilometers\ninto space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777060,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But now, we see something no one has ever\nseen before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780780,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the briefest period, no more than a few\nhours, there are two moons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787320,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only in these most detailed simulations does\nthis happen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791880,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While the largest falls back into the earth,\nits gravity plays an important role in helping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798780,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "raise the smaller one into a wider, stabler\norbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802320,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These simulations show that in just under\ntwo days’ time, Theia can hit the earth,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807660,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make two satellites, and then destroy one\nwhile leaving the other to become our moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813660,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s very different to the previous picture\nof a ring of material pulling together over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818700,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many months."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819420,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The new simulation gives us something very\nlike our own system - a moon that’s around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1% of the Earth’s mass, with an outer layer\nheated to 4000K - plenty to give us our magma"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830520,
    "dDurationMs": 240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ocean."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831720,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The surface of this virtual moon contains\na lot more material from proto-earth than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837120,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other models, while the interior is almost\nentirely from Theia."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841080,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However the iron content is low, just like\nin the real moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845400,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, like I said."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846720,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One minute there’s no moon - just a giant\nplanet hurtling towards you - and a few hours"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852060,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "later the moon is in place - according to\nthis simulation, with all the properties of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858660,
    "dDurationMs": 780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the actual moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860760,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course, there’s still a lot of uncertainty."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862920,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just because a few simulations give us something\nthat looks like the moon doesn’t mean this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867900,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is exactly what happened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868860,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But given the quality of the simulation, we\ncould argue that this is a more plausible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874560,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scenario than the earlier ideas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877440,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine what we could learn if we threw even\nmore resources at the problem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881040,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, we could add magnetic fields,\nwhich both bodies should have had prior to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886440,
    "dDurationMs": 300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collision."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886740,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll never have a perfect description of\nthe moon’s formation, but as evidence mounts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892320,
    "dDurationMs": 4233,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and simulations get better we can continue\nto narrow down the range of possibilities"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896553,
    "dDurationMs": 4407,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the moon’s formation until we’re left\nwith a general picture of what likely happened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901740,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if these simulations lead to predictions\n- like, for example, the nature of the moon’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906360,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interior - then we can test our theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908880,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps soon we’ll be able to confirm that\ncosmic cataclysm led to the weirdest moon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915660,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in our local patch of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918780,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d like to thank Speakly for supporting\nPBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921780,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If time travel were invented and you thought\n“Great!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925020,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now I can go back to the 1920’s and debate\ninterpretations of quantum mechanics with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928860,
    "dDurationMs": 1070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein and Schrödinger!”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929930,
    "dDurationMs": 2290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– well, then you’d need to learn German."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932220,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unless you already know it… but even in\nthis current time period, if you’ve wanted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936900,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to learn another language, then I’d like\nto introduce you to Speakly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940680,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Offering 8 different languages to choose from,\nSpeakly was created by 2 polyglots who both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946020,
    "dDurationMs": 900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speak 7 languages."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946920,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They researched thousands of language learners\nand created a unique method that teaches words"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952980,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and sentences based on their relevance in\nreal-life situations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956220,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This means that you don’t learn anything\nthat you actually can’t use to speak the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961380,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "language."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961860,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Available on both web and mobile platforms,\nyou’ll learn new vocabulary, with speaking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966900,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exercises, writing exercises, \nlistening comprehension "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969600,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exercises, and even music recommendations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971820,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the language that you’re learning so\nthat you don’t get bored."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974760,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a link in the description to learn\nmore."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977340,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hello and happy new year!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979320,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m excited to get into another year of\ntrying to understand this ridiculous universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984480,
    "dDurationMs": 420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985740,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another good place to try to understand the\nuniverse is Curt Jaimungal’s wonderful Theories"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991380,
    "dDurationMs": 780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Everything podcast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993060,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As it happens, he interviewed me recently\non topics ranging from consciousness and free"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997500,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will to godel incompleteness and dualities\nin physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000980,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I get quite a bit more opinionated than I\nusually do on Space Time, so it’s at least"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006380,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "colourful, and perhaps even interesting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008600,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you want to, you could also \nlet Curt know in the comments  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012080,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that space time sent you. Link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014720,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, today we’re doing comment responses\nfor the episode on supercritical fluids - that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020420,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bizarre hybrid state of matter between liquid\nand gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024440,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mitchblahman13 asks if I can clarify what\nliquid metallic hydrogen is - one of the states"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030920,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of hydrogen in the gas giants."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032600,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sure thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033560,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Firstly, solid metallic hydrogen is a theoretical\nstate of hydrogen at very high pressures in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1039620,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which H2 molecules form a lattice with metallic\nproperties - namely the electrons can travel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1044900,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "freely through the lattice."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046820,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Liquid metallic hydrogen is when you have\nthe same electron-sharing properties due to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050540,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the high density, but the temperature is high\nenough to break the lattice bonds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053600,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It still has the high conductivity of a metal,\nbut can flow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058100,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ChaosPotato and Jason Bouvette ask similar\nquestions: could you swim or float in an open-top"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1064040,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boat on an ocean of supercritical fluid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067640,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "My intuition says no, unfortunately."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069380,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Swimming requires substantial viscosity so\nyou have something to push against to move"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1074000,
    "dDurationMs": 240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forward."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1074240,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Supercritical fluids have viscosities closer\nto gases, so to swim you’d need really gigantic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1080360,
    "dDurationMs": 420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hands."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1080780,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although some animals do have hands large\nenough to swim in a gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085820,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re called birds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087800,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An open-top boat probably wouldn’t work\nbecause the boundary between the supercritical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1092240,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ocean and the gaseous atmosphere is not well\ndefined."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095300,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The buoyancy of such a boat comes from the\nfact that it’s filled with the medium of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1099560,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the atmosphere, which needs to be substantially\nless dense that the medium of the ocean - enough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104240,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a difference to make up for the fact that\nthe material of the boat itself is more dense"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108920,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than both media."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1110480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in the blurry supercritical fluid-gas\nboundary, the change in density between the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1115520,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "base and rim of the boat is relatively small."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1117920,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in order to float the boat has to be large\ncompared to the transition scale between media,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1123740,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which I think means gigantic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1126380,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or you could have a closed surface filled\nwith lower-density gas, in which case you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130700,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could easily float at the boundary."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1132320,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This would be something between a zeppelin\nand a submarine, which sounds super cool and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1137780,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "steampunk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1138260,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Roland Pihlakas requests that I please briefly\nsummarise again, how is supercritical fluid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1143300,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different from gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1144020,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Happy to, Roland."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1145460,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The main difference is density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Density is liquid-like, while viscosity and\nabsence of surface tension is gas-like."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1154280,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The high density means it interacts much more\nreadily with whatever its in contact with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1158480,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than a gas does due to the large number of\nparticles per unit volume, and can carry with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1163580,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it far more dissolved matter and heat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1166160,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The low viscosity and surface tension means\nit can get into tiny spaces much more easily"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1171320,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than a liquid - allowing it to actually get\nto the particles you want to dissolve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1175640,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Together, these properties make supercritical\nfluids amazing solvants, oxydizers, heat carriers,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1181100,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "etc. as well as making them extremely corrosive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1184160,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lord Marcus asks if there’s a supercritical\nphase where solids become indistinguishable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1188480,
    "dDurationMs": 780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from liquids."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1189860,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gareth Dean correctly responds - not really,\nbecause liquids and gases are both fluids"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1194720,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- no rigid bonds between particles, which "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1197960,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means an intermediate state between \nliquids and gases is meaningful."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1202460,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Solids, however, do have rigid bonds which\nmeans they can’t flow, and so there’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1206300,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no true intermediate state with liquids."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1208940,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That said, there are cases where the solid\nand liquid states are so mixed that the effect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1213740,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feels like a hybrid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1214760,
    "dDurationMs": 6870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Examples include gels - a crystal lattice\nof fiber networks filled with fluid, or colloids"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1221630,
    "dDurationMs": 3630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- aka slimes - solid particulates suspended\nin a liquid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1225260,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, that whole thing about glass really\nbeing a liquid is false."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1230060,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s an amorphous solid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1231560,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the topic of solid-liquid hybrids, Vitaliy\nVuychych and Sascha Wust point out that cats"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1237260,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are simultaneously solid and liquid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1241220,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While it’s true that cats can be brought\ninto an equilibrium between solid and liquid,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1246140,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this means raising their temperature to the\ncat melting point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1250880,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While that has been shown to place the cat\nin a state of being simultaneously solid and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1254720,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "liquid, it’s no longer in a state of being\nsimultaneously alive and dead."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1259100,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s just one of those."
    } ]
  } ]
}
